1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:05,365
2018. One spotlight on a television stage.
Allison Ellsworth is pitching her

2
00:00:05,365 --> 00:00:09,850
vinegar soda to five investors... and she
is nine months pregnant.

3
00:00:09,990 --> 00:00:15,030
In the one lit chair: Rohan Oza. He is about to
offer her four hundred thousand

4
00:00:15,030 --> 00:00:15,540
dollars.

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,310
Psst. Don't get too attached to me.

6
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:21,990
We know what they don't. One day, a cola
giant will pay one point nine five

7
00:00:21,990 --> 00:00:23,850
billion dollars for this drink.

8
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,929
Two billion dollars? For vinegar?

9
00:00:26,069 --> 00:00:27,759
Same soda. Keep watching.

10
00:00:27,899 --> 00:00:29,129
Is she okay up there?

11
00:00:29,269 --> 00:00:31,009
She's fine. I'm the one sweating.

12
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:33,819
Her son will be born ten days
after this pitch.

13
00:00:33,959 --> 00:00:37,419
And that big number has a part
almost nobody noticed.

14
00:00:37,559 --> 00:00:41,829
So how did Poppi make one point nine five
billion dollars from soda?

15
00:00:41,969 --> 00:00:47,350
It starts around 2015, in a family kitchen.
Allison has stomach troubles. So she

16
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:50,579
mixes apple cider vinegar, fruit
and soda water.

17
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:55,160
Her husband, Stephen, helps sell it at
farmers' markets in Dallas. Within

18
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:58,019
months, a Whole Foods buyer
agrees to stock it.

19
00:00:58,199 --> 00:00:59,499
Vinegar. In a soda.

20
00:00:59,639 --> 00:01:00,249
With fruit!

21
00:01:00,389 --> 00:01:01,889
Who drinks vinegar on purpose?

22
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:03,029
People with stomachs?

23
00:01:03,169 --> 00:01:04,129
...Fine. You're in.

24
00:01:04,309 --> 00:01:08,923
The first eighteen months bring in about five
hundred thousand dollars of sales.

25
00:01:08,923 --> 00:01:10,249
Growing costs far more.

26
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:15,249
Allison later told the Wall Street Journal, as
Fortune reported: "So we maxed

27
00:01:15,249 --> 00:01:19,099
out our credit cards, sold one of our
cars to buy bottles"...

28
00:01:19,239 --> 00:01:23,322
...and opened their own factory. Her
husband, she said, told her she was

29
00:01:23,322 --> 00:01:24,399
"absolutely crazy".

30
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:27,439
Credit cards. A car. A factory.

31
00:01:27,579 --> 00:01:28,309
Is that a lot?

32
00:01:28,449 --> 00:01:30,789
For a family? That's everything.

33
00:01:30,929 --> 00:01:31,769
And if I don't sell?

34
00:01:31,909 --> 00:01:34,899
Then you're very expensive salad dressing.

35
00:01:35,079 --> 00:01:39,345
Then they practised one pitch for six months...
for a stage with five investors

36
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:39,669
on it.

37
00:01:40,809 --> 00:01:43,659
Six months of practice. Then the
lights came on.

38
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:48,349
The ask: four hundred thousand dollars for ten
percent of the company. That puts

39
00:01:48,349 --> 00:01:50,169
the whole thing at four million.

40
00:01:50,309 --> 00:01:55,259
Accounts of the episode say the investors pushed
hard. One worried how much more

41
00:01:55,259 --> 00:02:00,148
money it would need. One disliked the
valuation. Others objected to the plan to

42
00:02:00,148 --> 00:02:01,509
run their own factory.

43
00:02:01,689 --> 00:02:02,989
No. No. No. No...

44
00:02:03,129 --> 00:02:03,879
That's four.

45
00:02:04,019 --> 00:02:05,349
...Anyone? Anyone at all?

46
00:02:05,529 --> 00:02:09,716
The fifth chair was Rohan Oza's. Four
hundred thousand dollars... for

47
00:02:09,716 --> 00:02:10,929
twenty-five percent.

48
00:02:11,069 --> 00:02:16,133
Same money, two and a half times the share. By
our arithmetic, his offer valued

49
00:02:16,133 --> 00:02:19,659
the company at one point six
million dollars, not four.

50
00:02:19,839 --> 00:02:21,526
Four million, bro. Ten percent.

51
00:02:21,666 --> 00:02:24,046
One point six. A quarter.

52
00:02:24,186 --> 00:02:25,026
That's robbery!

53
00:02:25,166 --> 00:02:26,356
That's a deal.

54
00:02:26,496 --> 00:02:27,226
...I'll take it.

55
00:02:27,406 --> 00:02:31,651
She took it. She remembers thinking, "this
moment right here could change my

56
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:31,986
life."

57
00:02:32,126 --> 00:02:34,766
Ten days after the pitch, her
son was born.

58
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:36,406
Visiting hours are over.

59
00:02:36,546 --> 00:02:37,816
I'm family. Sort of.

60
00:02:37,956 --> 00:02:38,836
You're a beverage.

61
00:02:39,016 --> 00:02:43,106
The episode aired on Sunday the
ninth of December, 2018.

62
00:02:43,286 --> 00:02:45,586
Twenty-five percent of a vinegar company.

63
00:02:45,726 --> 00:02:46,646
Is that good?

64
00:02:46,786 --> 00:02:49,326
Depends what the other
seventy-five turns into.

65
00:02:49,466 --> 00:02:50,596
When do we find out?

66
00:02:50,736 --> 00:02:51,956
Ask me in six years.

67
00:02:52,136 --> 00:02:55,436
Years later, Oza recalled what he
had seen in that pitch.

68
00:02:55,576 --> 00:03:00,140
"Package, wrong. Brand, wrong. Everything is
wrong, basically. But this liquid,

69
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:00,776
that's it."

70
00:03:00,956 --> 00:03:02,376
Label, wrong. Bottle, wrong.

71
00:03:02,516 --> 00:03:02,966
And me?

72
00:03:03,106 --> 00:03:03,836
You, I keep.

73
00:03:04,016 --> 00:03:06,876
He had bought the drink. Now he
wanted a new word on it.

74
00:03:08,016 --> 00:03:09,856
The first thing to go was the name.

75
00:03:09,996 --> 00:03:12,906
According to Mashed, "Mother" could
not be trademarked.

76
00:03:13,086 --> 00:03:14,596
I'd like to own my name, please.

77
00:03:14,736 --> 00:03:16,186
Not that one.

78
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:17,316
...What about my face?

79
00:03:17,456 --> 00:03:18,366
Next.

80
00:03:18,546 --> 00:03:23,146
Later, Oza put it more politely: "The name and
package were not conducive of the

81
00:03:23,146 --> 00:03:25,446
big picture that we're trying to solve."

82
00:03:25,586 --> 00:03:31,191
So in 2020 the bottle became a can. The name
became Poppi. The label said

83
00:03:31,191 --> 00:03:32,496
"prebiotic soda".

84
00:03:32,676 --> 00:03:34,176
Same soda. New word.

85
00:03:34,316 --> 00:03:35,616
You look... expensive.

86
00:03:35,756 --> 00:03:36,686
I feel expensive.

87
00:03:36,826 --> 00:03:37,916
You taste the same.

88
00:03:38,056 --> 00:03:38,916
Don't tell anyone.

89
00:03:39,096 --> 00:03:44,037
Oza's firm, CAVU Consumer Partners, backed
it. PepsiCo would later say CAVU

90
00:03:44,037 --> 00:03:46,606
funded Poppi from its first seed round.

91
00:03:46,746 --> 00:03:51,576
The launch was set for March 2020... at a
trade show that Covid cancelled.

92
00:03:51,756 --> 00:03:52,636
Where is everybody?

93
00:03:52,776 --> 00:03:54,246
Home. Everybody's home.

94
00:03:54,386 --> 00:03:55,716
Then I'm going to their homes.

95
00:03:55,896 --> 00:04:01,791
So they sold online, through Amazon and online
grocers. By the end of 2020, one

96
00:04:01,791 --> 00:04:05,596
report says, sales were about
four million dollars.

97
00:04:05,736 --> 00:04:10,493
Then came one video. Food Republic reports
that a TikTok of Allison added a

98
00:04:10,493 --> 00:04:12,966
hundred thousand in sales within a day.

99
00:04:13,146 --> 00:04:14,416
Bro. The can is viral.

100
00:04:14,556 --> 00:04:15,261
I'm a soda.

101
00:04:15,401 --> 00:04:16,871
Not anymore. Now you're content.

102
00:04:17,011 --> 00:04:17,721
Is that better?

103
00:04:17,861 --> 00:04:18,571
It's louder.

104
00:04:18,751 --> 00:04:23,576
But sales are not cash. Allison told
Entrepreneur that at twenty million dollars

105
00:04:23,576 --> 00:04:28,160
of revenue, the company had seventy-five
thousand dollars in the bank... and

106
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,211
wasn't sure it could make payroll.

107
00:04:30,351 --> 00:04:34,881
Stephen remembers "begging and pleading" with
suppliers for more time to pay.

108
00:04:35,061 --> 00:04:36,301
Pay Friday.

109
00:04:36,441 --> 00:04:37,751
How about... a Friday?

110
00:04:37,891 --> 00:04:38,841
Which one?

111
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:41,921
They needed money that didn't come
from a credit card.

112
00:04:43,061 --> 00:04:45,931
The money came... with famous
faces attached.

113
00:04:46,071 --> 00:04:51,467
In August 2021, Poppi raised thirteen
million dollars, led by Oza's firm.

114
00:04:51,467 --> 00:04:57,086
Celebrities such as Halsey and Russell
Westbrook joined in, Dallas Innovates

115
00:04:57,086 --> 00:04:57,751
reported.

116
00:04:57,931 --> 00:04:58,951
Can in hand. Chin up.

117
00:04:59,091 --> 00:05:00,711
Does anyone actually drink me?

118
00:05:00,851 --> 00:05:02,474
Nobody sips, bro. They post.

119
00:05:02,654 --> 00:05:07,554
The cans sold for two dollars forty-nine each,
in more than seven thousand five

120
00:05:07,554 --> 00:05:08,484
hundred stores.

121
00:05:08,664 --> 00:05:10,541
Two forty-nine. For one can.

122
00:05:10,681 --> 00:05:11,931
You're not buying a soda.

123
00:05:12,071 --> 00:05:13,321
Then what am I buying?

124
00:05:13,461 --> 00:05:14,861
A picture of you holding it.

125
00:05:15,001 --> 00:05:16,301
...It is a nice picture.

126
00:05:16,481 --> 00:05:22,200
In December 2022, another twenty-five million
dollars. Sales had grown a hundred

127
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:24,631
and forty-eight percent in a year.

128
00:05:24,771 --> 00:05:30,655
By 2024, the company says, revenue was about
five hundred million dollars. By

129
00:05:30,655 --> 00:05:34,781
our arithmetic, that's more than a
hundred times 2020.

130
00:05:34,961 --> 00:05:36,721
Four million to five hundred million!

131
00:05:36,861 --> 00:05:38,481
Sales. Not profit.

132
00:05:38,621 --> 00:05:39,341
Same thing!

133
00:05:39,481 --> 00:05:40,641
It is not.

134
00:05:40,821 --> 00:05:46,143
Then the biggest stage of all: the Super Bowl,
on the ninth of February 2025.

135
00:05:46,143 --> 00:05:47,871
You may have seen the ad.

136
00:05:48,051 --> 00:05:50,011
What does a Super Bowl ad even cost?

137
00:05:50,151 --> 00:05:52,050
Doesn't matter. Everybody saw the can.

138
00:05:52,190 --> 00:05:53,530
I was looking at the snacks.

139
00:05:53,710 --> 00:05:58,479
Before its ad, Poppi sent branded vending
machines to thirty-two influencers.

140
00:05:58,619 --> 00:06:03,395
A rival brand, Olipop, posted that each
machine cost twenty-five thousand

141
00:06:03,395 --> 00:06:05,619
dollars. Keep that number in mind.

142
00:06:05,799 --> 00:06:07,409
Twenty-five grand for a fridge?

143
00:06:07,549 --> 00:06:08,419
Less! Way less!

144
00:06:08,559 --> 00:06:09,409
How much less?

145
00:06:09,549 --> 00:06:10,059
...Less.

146
00:06:10,239 --> 00:06:15,187
A Poppi spokesperson called the figure "not
only fabricated but inflated by

147
00:06:15,187 --> 00:06:20,267
sixty percent". Allison posted a TikTok
blaming "misinformation" from "one of

148
00:06:20,267 --> 00:06:21,389
our competitors".

149
00:06:21,529 --> 00:06:26,668
A fight about a fridge. But the fridge was not
the point. And PepsiCo's own try

150
00:06:26,668 --> 00:06:28,619
was about to be reported dead.

151
00:06:29,759 --> 00:06:32,029
Because PepsiCo had tried to build one.

152
00:06:32,169 --> 00:06:37,779
Its own prebiotic soda was called Soulboost.
In March 2025, days before the

153
00:06:37,779 --> 00:06:41,219
deal, Bloomberg reported it had
been scrapped.

154
00:06:41,399 --> 00:06:43,889
Fiber. Fizz. Fruit. Perfect.

155
00:06:44,029 --> 00:06:45,549
And the cool kids?

156
00:06:45,689 --> 00:06:46,589
They'll come.

157
00:06:46,729 --> 00:06:47,679
Build one?

158
00:06:47,819 --> 00:06:48,559
Tried that.

159
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:49,719
Borrow one?

160
00:06:49,859 --> 00:06:51,189
Nobody lends you a can.

161
00:06:51,329 --> 00:06:52,669
Then... buy one.

162
00:06:52,849 --> 00:06:58,731
Coca-Cola had launched its own, called Simply
Pop. And Olipop, the brand behind

163
00:06:58,731 --> 00:07:04,612
the fridge post, was valued at one point eight
five billion dollars in February

164
00:07:04,612 --> 00:07:06,399
2025, according to CNBC.

165
00:07:06,539 --> 00:07:12,307
Olipop's chief executive, Ben Goodwin, had
said in 2023 that both PepsiCo and

166
00:07:12,307 --> 00:07:15,379
Coca-Cola had approached it about a sale.

167
00:07:15,559 --> 00:07:16,799
Will you be mine?

168
00:07:16,939 --> 00:07:18,009
Me? I'm vinegar.

169
00:07:18,149 --> 00:07:19,729
You're popular vinegar.

170
00:07:19,869 --> 00:07:20,579
How popular?

171
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:22,419
Expensive popular.

172
00:07:22,599 --> 00:07:27,502
PepsiCo's chief executive, Ramon Laguarta,
would later say why it wanted one:

173
00:07:27,502 --> 00:07:31,896
consumers, he said, have a "growing
interest in health and wellness."

174
00:07:32,076 --> 00:07:33,756
Health... It used to say gut health?

175
00:07:33,896 --> 00:07:35,944
Shh. Not in front of the lawyers.

176
00:07:36,084 --> 00:07:37,023
What lawyers?

177
00:07:37,163 --> 00:07:37,923
...Those ones.

178
00:07:38,103 --> 00:07:42,756
Health was the word everyone wanted. But on
Poppi's can, that word was already

179
00:07:42,756 --> 00:07:43,293
in court.

180
00:07:44,433 --> 00:07:49,970
In May 2024, a shopper filed a class action
in federal court in California.

181
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:53,883
Three more suits followed, all
before the same judge.

182
00:07:54,023 --> 00:07:58,508
According to the lawsuit, a can held about two
grams of prebiotic fiber... so

183
00:07:58,508 --> 00:08:03,051
you'd need more than four cans a day for any
effect, with the sugar that comes

184
00:08:03,051 --> 00:08:03,633
with them.

185
00:08:03,813 --> 00:08:06,033
Two grams of fiber?

186
00:08:06,173 --> 00:08:06,813
Allegedly.

187
00:08:06,953 --> 00:08:08,943
Four cans a day?

188
00:08:09,083 --> 00:08:09,743
Allegedly!

189
00:08:09,923 --> 00:08:14,863
Poppi denied all of it. It says its label
claims were true. It told Fortune a

190
00:08:14,863 --> 00:08:18,263
can has three grams of fiber and
five grams of sugar.

191
00:08:18,403 --> 00:08:24,041
In its own words, it had also removed "any and
all references to gut health"

192
00:08:24,041 --> 00:08:26,193
from its labels in late 2023.

193
00:08:26,373 --> 00:08:28,058
Same soda. Fewer words.

194
00:08:28,198 --> 00:08:29,538
Funny. Tastes the same.

195
00:08:29,678 --> 00:08:30,490
It is the same.

196
00:08:30,630 --> 00:08:31,910
Then what were the words for?

197
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:37,613
On the fourth of December 2024, a day-long
mediation. On the sixteenth, the

198
00:08:37,613 --> 00:08:40,780
judge paused the case: a deal
in principle.

199
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:42,250
Your side?

200
00:08:42,390 --> 00:08:44,430
Not guilty. But tired.

201
00:08:44,570 --> 00:08:45,850
Their side?

202
00:08:45,990 --> 00:08:47,330
Also tired.

203
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:51,690
Three months later, Bloomberg would report
PepsiCo's Soulboost scrapped.

204
00:08:51,830 --> 00:08:56,140
The settlement: eight point nine million
dollars. Poppi admitted no fault.

205
00:08:56,280 --> 00:09:00,618
Buyers could claim about nine dollars for a
twelve-pack, and at least five

206
00:09:00,618 --> 00:09:01,790
dollars a household.

207
00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:02,830
Nine dollars.

208
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:04,102
For all that soda?

209
00:09:04,242 --> 00:09:05,982
For a can that talked about your gut.

210
00:09:06,122 --> 00:09:06,992
Allegedly.

211
00:09:07,132 --> 00:09:09,202
...Next time, read the can. Every word.

212
00:09:09,382 --> 00:09:14,572
Now the timing. The settlement was dated the
fifth of March 2025. It was filed

213
00:09:14,572 --> 00:09:19,762
in court on Friday the fourteenth. On Monday
the seventeenth, the buyer spoke.

214
00:09:19,942 --> 00:09:21,074
Friday to Monday?

215
00:09:21,214 --> 00:09:22,054
Tidy.

216
00:09:22,194 --> 00:09:23,024
Is that normal?

217
00:09:23,164 --> 00:09:24,274
It's tidy.

218
00:09:24,454 --> 00:09:28,544
The lawsuit was settled on paper. The buyer
could finally say its number.

219
00:09:29,684 --> 00:09:35,454
Monday, the seventeenth of March 2025. PepsiCo
announced it would buy Poppi.

220
00:09:35,634 --> 00:09:38,064
Sign here. And here. And here.

221
00:09:38,204 --> 00:09:39,034
What am I signing?

222
00:09:39,174 --> 00:09:40,064
Yourself.

223
00:09:40,244 --> 00:09:45,091
The price: one point nine five billion dollars.
By our arithmetic, that's four

224
00:09:45,091 --> 00:09:49,875
thousand eight hundred and seventy-five times
what Oza offered on that stage.

225
00:09:50,055 --> 00:09:51,625
That's a lot of briefcases.

226
00:09:51,765 --> 00:09:53,355
Same soda. Bigger number.

227
00:09:53,495 --> 00:09:54,435
How many are there?

228
00:09:54,575 --> 00:09:55,915
Don't count. Just look up.

229
00:09:56,095 --> 00:10:00,920
But read the press release. That figure
includes three hundred million dollars

230
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:03,085
of "anticipated cash tax benefits".

231
00:10:03,225 --> 00:10:08,102
Of every hundred dollars in the headline,
about fifteen is that tax benefit.

232
00:10:08,102 --> 00:10:12,915
PepsiCo calls the rest, one point six five
billion, the net purchase price.

233
00:10:13,095 --> 00:10:14,215
So it's not two billion?

234
00:10:14,355 --> 00:10:15,815
The headline is.

235
00:10:15,955 --> 00:10:16,645
And the rest?

236
00:10:16,785 --> 00:10:18,749
The rest is a promise.

237
00:10:18,929 --> 00:10:24,064
On top, PepsiCo offered an earnout: more money
later, if Poppi hit targets. The

238
00:10:24,064 --> 00:10:25,559
release gave no amount.

239
00:10:25,699 --> 00:10:29,539
The deal closed on the
nineteenth of May 2025.

240
00:10:29,679 --> 00:10:33,941
How much went to the Ellsworths? Nobody has
published the split. Fortune

241
00:10:33,941 --> 00:10:37,789
reported more than a hundred million
dollars. No filing shows it.

242
00:10:37,969 --> 00:10:39,239
Over a hundred million?

243
00:10:39,379 --> 00:10:40,469
Reported.

244
00:10:40,609 --> 00:10:42,409
I can't pay a bill with "reported".

245
00:10:42,589 --> 00:10:47,088
Allison told Fortune that selling had been the
plan "from day one". And with

246
00:10:47,088 --> 00:10:51,469
PepsiCo, she said, "Now we have a Ferrari
underneath us for distribution."

247
00:10:51,649 --> 00:10:52,169
Faster!

248
00:10:52,309 --> 00:10:54,129
Hold on to your label.

249
00:10:54,269 --> 00:10:54,849
Which one?

250
00:10:55,029 --> 00:10:58,589
But one piece of that price was never
cash. It was a promise.

251
00:10:59,729 --> 00:11:02,139
Here is the part almost nobody noticed.

252
00:11:02,279 --> 00:11:07,427
According to Bloomberg, that earnout could
reach three hundred million dollars,

253
00:11:07,427 --> 00:11:10,359
for targets due by the third
quarter of 2027.

254
00:11:10,499 --> 00:11:16,845
In December 2025, PepsiCo valued it at two
hundred and seventy-eight million. By

255
00:11:16,845 --> 00:11:23,191
June 2026, a hundred and seventeen million. As
of the fifth of September 2026, a

256
00:11:23,191 --> 00:11:25,809
filing said about twelve million.

257
00:11:25,989 --> 00:11:27,289
Is that... my bonus?

258
00:11:27,429 --> 00:11:28,817
What's left of it.

259
00:11:28,957 --> 00:11:30,457
It could have been three hundred million!

260
00:11:30,597 --> 00:11:31,507
Could have.

261
00:11:31,687 --> 00:11:37,672
By our arithmetic, of every hundred dollars
PepsiCo once expected to pay, it now

262
00:11:37,672 --> 00:11:42,385
expects about four. Bloomberg reported
the cut in October 2026.

263
00:11:42,525 --> 00:11:47,442
Ken Shea of Bloomberg Intelligence said that at
this rate, it "seems plausible"

264
00:11:47,442 --> 00:11:52,048
PepsiCo may not pay any of it. PepsiCo said
Poppi grew its sales volume by

265
00:11:52,048 --> 00:11:55,285
double digits in the third quarter
and gained share.

266
00:11:55,465 --> 00:11:56,615
Sales are up!

267
00:11:56,755 --> 00:11:58,285
Then why is the bonus down?

268
00:11:58,425 --> 00:11:59,815
Volume is up!

269
00:11:59,955 --> 00:12:01,195
That's not what I asked.

270
00:12:01,335 --> 00:12:02,585
...Next question.

271
00:12:02,765 --> 00:12:08,024
So where does this touch you? In your fridge.
The class action covered cans

272
00:12:08,024 --> 00:12:13,633
bought from late January 2020, and its claim
deadline closed on the twenty-sixth

273
00:12:13,633 --> 00:12:14,895
of September 2025.

274
00:12:15,075 --> 00:12:16,515
This one just says soda.

275
00:12:16,655 --> 00:12:17,565
Read the small print.

276
00:12:17,705 --> 00:12:18,795
It says... soda.

277
00:12:18,975 --> 00:12:23,336
And next time you stand in the soda aisle,
read the word on the can. Somebody

278
00:12:23,336 --> 00:12:24,865
paid a lot of money for it.

279
00:12:25,005 --> 00:12:30,060
The founders started again. On the ninth of
November 2026, they launched a new

280
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:35,114
drink, Jetty, at two forty-nine a bottle, paid
for with two and a half million

281
00:12:35,114 --> 00:12:36,475
dollars of their own.

282
00:12:36,615 --> 00:12:41,005
Allison told Modern Retail: "No one is
excited to see me win again."

283
00:12:41,185 --> 00:12:42,475
New bottle. New word.

284
00:12:42,615 --> 00:12:43,490
Same founders.

285
00:12:43,670 --> 00:12:48,611
The next date to watch: PepsiCo's next
quarterly filing, when the value of that

286
00:12:48,611 --> 00:12:50,050
bonus can change again.

287
00:12:50,230 --> 00:12:50,970
Same soda.

288
00:12:51,110 --> 00:12:52,049
Different word.

289
00:12:52,229 --> 00:12:57,293
So how did Poppi make one point nine five
billion dollars from soda? It changed

290
00:12:57,293 --> 00:13:02,229
the word on the can, not the soda... and the
number was never quite that big.

